1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,626
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reinforcement learning now drives safety-critical
systems like autonomous vehicles,</font>

2
00:00:04,706 --> 00:00:08,833
<font color="#FFFFFF">but adversarial perturbations to a policy's input
states can quietly steer it toward disaster.</font>

3
00:00:08,913 --> 00:00:13,039
<font color="#FFFFFF">Many defenses improve robustness empirically,</font>

4
00:00:13,119 --> 00:00:17,245
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet almost none can certify it with guarantees.</font>

5
00:00:17,325 --> 00:00:21,452
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces CROP,</font>

6
00:00:21,532 --> 00:00:25,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first unified framework to certify robust
policies for reinforcement learning through functional smoothing.</font>

7
00:00:25,738 --> 00:00:29,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">CROP certifies robustness at two levels: the
stability of the action taken at each state,</font>

8
00:00:29,945 --> 00:00:34,071
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a provable lower bound on the cumulative reward
across a whole trajectory.</font>

9
00:00:34,151 --> 00:00:38,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using it,</font>

10
00:00:38,357 --> 00:00:42,484
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors benchmark nine existing robust RL
algorithms across four environments and</font>

11
00:00:42,564 --> 00:00:46,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">show that their certificates are often tight.</font>

12
00:00:47,070 --> 00:00:51,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reinforcement learning has moved into domains where
failure is costly,</font>

13
00:00:51,599 --> 00:00:54,042
<font color="#FFFFFF">such as autonomous driving and trading.</font>

14
00:00:54,122 --> 00:00:59,801
<font color="#FFFFFF">But researchers have shown that an adversary who
slightly perturbs the state observations</font>

15
00:00:59,881 --> 00:01:03,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">fed to an RL agent can reliably change its decisions.</font>

16
00:01:03,310 --> 00:01:08,729
<font color="#FFFFFF">A wave of empirical defenses followed, only to be
defeated by newer adaptive attacks.</font>

17
00:01:08,809 --> 00:01:13,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">What has been missing is certification: a way to
prove, rather than just observe,</font>

18
00:01:14,050 --> 00:01:19,534
<font color="#FFFFFF">that a trained policy stays reliable under every
perturbation within a bounded budget.</font>

19
00:01:19,614 --> 00:01:23,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper tackles exactly that gap for
reinforcement learning.</font>

20
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:27,835
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core motivation is trust.</font>

21
00:01:27,915 --> 00:01:31,761
<font color="#FFFFFF">If you cannot prove a policy is robust,</font>

22
00:01:31,841 --> 00:01:35,686
<font color="#FFFFFF">then passing today's attacks tells you little about
tomorrow's.</font>

23
00:01:35,766 --> 00:01:39,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">Randomized smoothing has become a leading tool for
certifying image classifiers,</font>

24
00:01:39,692 --> 00:01:43,537
<font color="#FFFFFF">but reinforcement learning does not fit its mold.</font>

25
00:01:43,617 --> 00:01:47,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">In classification the confidence output lives in a
known zero-to-one range and behaves like a probability;</font>

26
00:01:47,543 --> 00:01:51,388
<font color="#FFFFFF">in Q-learning the value function has an unknown
range and its outputs are not probabilities at all.</font>

27
00:01:51,468 --> 00:01:55,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of that,</font>

28
00:01:55,394 --> 00:01:59,239
<font color="#FFFFFF">a single action decision is not the whole story:
what ultimately matters is the reward</font>

29
00:01:59,319 --> 00:02:03,165
<font color="#FFFFFF">accumulated along an entire trajectory of decisions.</font>

30
00:02:03,245 --> 00:02:07,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">CROP is designed to overcome both obstacles.</font>

31
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:11,322
<font color="#FFFFFF">CROP makes three main contributions.</font>

32
00:02:11,402 --> 00:02:15,253
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

33
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:19,185
<font color="#FFFFFF">it defines two certification criteria for
reinforcement learning: robustness of the per-state action,</font>

34
00:02:19,265 --> 00:02:23,117
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a lower bound on the cumulative reward.</font>

35
00:02:23,197 --> 00:02:27,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it turns each criterion into an algorithm.</font>

36
00:02:27,128 --> 00:02:30,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">CROP-LoAct uses local randomized smoothing to
certify a radius around each state within</font>

37
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:34,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">which the chosen action cannot change.</font>

38
00:02:34,992 --> 00:02:38,843
<font color="#FFFFFF">CROP-GRe uses global smoothing to bound the
expected and percentile reward,</font>

39
00:02:38,923 --> 00:02:42,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">and CROP-LoRe performs an adaptive tree search to
produce a much tighter absolute lower bound on reward.</font>

40
00:02:42,855 --> 00:02:46,707
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

41
00:02:46,787 --> 00:02:50,638
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors apply these tools to nine existing
robust RL algorithms across four environments,</font>

42
00:02:50,718 --> 00:02:54,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">and release the results as an open leaderboard for
the community.</font>

43
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:57,573
<font color="#FFFFFF">The engine of CROP is functional smoothing.</font>

44
00:02:57,653 --> 00:02:59,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">At each state, and for each action,</font>

45
00:02:59,854 --> 00:03:05,306
<font color="#FFFFFF">the method adds Gaussian noise to the state and
averages the trained Q-network's output,</font>

46
00:03:05,386 --> 00:03:07,570
<font color="#FFFFFF">producing a smoothed value function.</font>

47
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:11,656
<font color="#FFFFFF">A key lemma shows this smoothed function is
Lipschitz continuous,</font>

48
00:03:11,736 --> 00:03:15,491
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a constant that shrinks as the smoothing
variance grows.</font>

49
00:03:15,571 --> 00:03:16,812
<font color="#FFFFFF">From that continuity,</font>

50
00:03:16,892 --> 00:03:22,596
<font color="#FFFFFF">Theorem 1 gives a certified radius: as long as the
perturbation is smaller than this radius,</font>

51
00:03:22,676 --> 00:03:25,425
<font color="#FFFFFF">the smoothed policy's action does not change.</font>

52
00:03:25,505 --> 00:03:30,706
<font color="#FFFFFF">The radius depends on the gap between the best and
runner-up smoothed action-values,</font>

53
00:03:30,786 --> 00:03:32,089
<font color="#FFFFFF">revealing a trade-off,</font>

54
00:03:32,169 --> 00:03:36,678
<font color="#FFFFFF">since more smoothing stabilizes the values but also
narrows their margin.</font>

55
00:03:36,758 --> 00:03:38,062
<font color="#FFFFFF">For cumulative reward,</font>

56
00:03:38,142 --> 00:03:44,537
<font color="#FFFFFF">global smoothing treats the whole trajectory as a
function to bound the expected and percentile reward,</font>

57
00:03:44,617 --> 00:03:51,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the adaptive local search of CROP-LoRe grows
a trajectory tree to certify a tight absolute lower bound.</font>

58
00:03:51,770 --> 00:03:55,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test the framework broadly,</font>

59
00:03:55,828 --> 00:03:59,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors run it on four environments spanning
very different regimes.</font>

60
00:03:59,886 --> 00:04:03,864
<font color="#FFFFFF">Pong and Freeway are high-dimensional Atari games;</font>

61
00:04:03,944 --> 00:04:07,922
<font color="#FFFFFF">CartPole is a classic low-dimensional control task;</font>

62
00:04:08,002 --> 00:04:11,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Highway simulates autonomous driving.</font>

63
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:16,038
<font color="#FFFFFF">On these, they certify nine existing reinforcement
learning methods,</font>

64
00:04:16,118 --> 00:04:20,096
<font color="#FFFFFF">ranging from standard training and Gaussian
augmentation to adversarial training,</font>

65
00:04:20,176 --> 00:04:24,154
<font color="#FFFFFF">the SA-MDP variants, RadialRL, CARRL, NoisyNet, and
gradient-based DQN.</font>

66
00:04:24,234 --> 00:04:28,212
<font color="#FFFFFF">This makes CROP not just a single certificate but a
benchmark that places many robust RL methods on a common,</font>

67
00:04:28,292 --> 00:04:32,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">provable footing.</font>

68
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:36,296
<font color="#FFFFFF">The certifications reveal clear and consistent winners.</font>

69
00:04:36,376 --> 00:04:40,022
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Freeway,</font>

70
00:04:40,102 --> 00:04:43,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">RadialRL achieves the highest certified radius
across every smoothing level,</font>

71
00:04:43,828 --> 00:04:47,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">because it explicitly optimizes against worst-case
perturbations.</font>

72
00:04:47,553 --> 00:04:51,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Pong, SA-MDP with the convex relaxation is the
most certifiably robust.</font>

73
00:04:51,279 --> 00:04:54,925
<font color="#FFFFFF">Encouragingly,</font>

74
00:04:55,005 --> 00:04:58,651
<font color="#FFFFFF">these certified rankings largely agree with what
people had observed empirically,</font>

75
00:04:58,731 --> 00:05:02,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">which builds confidence in the certificates.</font>

76
00:05:02,457 --> 00:05:06,103
<font color="#FFFFFF">The analysis also surfaces new structure: on Pong
every method shows a periodic pattern</font>

77
00:05:06,183 --> 00:05:09,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">in its certified radius over time,</font>

78
00:05:09,908 --> 00:05:13,554
<font color="#FFFFFF">with robustness peaking at confident states such as
when the ball flies toward the paddle,</font>

79
00:05:13,634 --> 00:05:17,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">an insight that could guide future robust training.</font>

80
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:21,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">A central ablation studies the smoothing variance sigma.</font>

81
00:05:22,042 --> 00:05:26,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Freeway,</font>

82
00:05:26,424 --> 00:05:30,726
<font color="#FFFFFF">robustness for the strong methods keeps improving
as sigma grows all the way to one point zero,</font>

83
00:05:30,806 --> 00:05:35,108
<font color="#FFFFFF">since Freeway tolerates large noise.</font>

84
00:05:35,188 --> 00:05:39,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">On Pong the story differs: too much smoothing hurts,</font>

85
00:05:39,570 --> 00:05:43,872
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a moderate sigma between about zero point zero
one and zero point zero three works best for nearly all methods.</font>

86
00:05:43,952 --> 00:05:48,254
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors also compare their three reward bounds.</font>

87
00:05:48,334 --> 00:05:52,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">The percentile bound is far tighter than the loose
expectation bound,</font>

88
00:05:52,716 --> 00:05:57,018
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the absolute lower bound from CROP-LoRe often
matches the empirical reward under PGD attack exactly,</font>

89
00:05:57,098 --> 00:06:01,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">a zero gap that demonstrates the certificates are
tight rather than merely valid.</font>

90
00:06:01,780 --> 00:06:03,894
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the scope.</font>

91
00:06:03,974 --> 00:06:09,172
<font color="#FFFFFF">CROP is the first unified certification framework
for reinforcement learning,</font>

92
00:06:09,252 --> 00:06:13,627
<font color="#FFFFFF">working at both the action level and the
cumulative-reward level.</font>

93
00:06:13,707 --> 00:06:19,454
<font color="#FFFFFF">It certifies nine existing robust RL methods across
four environments: Pong, Freeway,</font>

94
00:06:19,534 --> 00:06:20,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">CartPole, and Highway.</font>

95
00:06:21,042 --> 00:06:24,869
<font color="#FFFFFF">It does so with three algorithms: CROP-LoAct for actions,</font>

96
00:06:24,949 --> 00:06:27,474
<font color="#FFFFFF">and CROP-GRe and CROP-LoRe for reward.</font>

97
00:06:27,554 --> 00:06:32,272
<font color="#FFFFFF">The smoothing variance is pushed as high as one
point zero on Freeway,</font>

98
00:06:32,352 --> 00:06:37,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">and reward robustness is reported through three
bounds, an expectation bound,</font>

99
00:06:37,630 --> 00:06:41,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">a fifty-percent percentile bound, and an absolute
lower bound.</font>

100
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:48,349
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message of CROP is that robustness in
reinforcement learning need not be a matter of hope.</font>

101
00:06:48,429 --> 00:06:50,310
<font color="#FFFFFF">By smoothing the value function,</font>

102
00:06:50,390 --> 00:06:54,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">you can prove that an agent's action stays fixed
within a certified radius,</font>

103
00:06:54,985 --> 00:07:00,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">and you can prove a lower bound on the reward it
will collect under any bounded attack.</font>

104
00:07:00,315 --> 00:07:03,238
<font color="#FFFFFF">Applied to nine methods across four environments,</font>

105
00:07:03,318 --> 00:07:06,669
<font color="#FFFFFF">these certificates are not only correct but often tight,</font>

106
00:07:06,749 --> 00:07:08,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">matching what attacks achieve.</font>

107
00:07:08,587 --> 00:07:13,163
<font color="#FFFFFF">CROP turns robust RL into something you can measure
and compare on a common,</font>

108
00:07:13,243 --> 00:07:14,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">provable leaderboard,</font>

109
00:07:14,530 --> 00:07:19,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">and invites the community to certify more methods
and more environments in future work.</font>
